dhcp: Do not report ports of a network with no dnsmasq

DhcpLocalProcess.enable logged a dnsmasq start timeout and returned
None, so _call_driver reported success, the agent cached the network,
and the network's ports were reported to the server as DHCP-ready while
nothing was serving them. DhcpBase.restart dropped enable's result, so a
failed restart went the same way.

enable now returns False, and restart returns what enable returned.
_call_driver treats that as a failed call: it drops the network's ports
from the sets waiting to be reported, schedules a resync for the
network, and returns False.

The callers that mark ports DHCP-ready now all honour that result:

* configure_dhcp_for_network returns whether the network's DHCP service
  is running, and neither caches the network nor marks its ports ready
  when it is not.
* refresh_dhcp_helper does not mark the ports ready when either of the
  driver calls it makes failed.
* reload_allocations, which promotes a change to the agent port's IP
  addresses into a restart, does not mark the port ready when that
  restart failed.
* sync_state reports the ports of the networks it configured rather than
  every port it knows, so a network that failed to start during the
  resync does not have its ports reported either.

A driver call that raised already meant the agent did not cache the
network; it now also means the network's ports are not reported.

No port is lost by this: the resync that each failure schedules reports
the network's ports once its DHCP service is running.

